ing about a  pass through the liver and this largely  applies to orally consumed products now  the most important enzyme family is  cytochrome p450 this is a large family  of liver enzymes that most  pharmaceutical drugs and cannabinoids  are processed through so there is a huge  variability in this enzyme between  populations you can have over a tenfold  difference between individuals and this  the the resources of variability and how  these enzyme works have largely to do  with two things one the drugs that are  there competing for it well as the  genetics what type of enzymes do you  have do you have the enzymes that work  really fast or do you have enzymes that  works really slow people inherit copies  of these and they can be combinations of  you know rapid metabolizers or regular  metabolize or slow metabolizers and  we'll show some examples of what happens  when you have two drugs competing at the  same set a little later on so for many  drugs that are distinct populations of  individuals with varying metabolizing  capacity  so in this little graph on the right you  can see this sort of breakdown and  obviously certain types are rare that  others the poor metabolizers tend to be  getting too bad copies let's just say  for conceptual purposes of one of these  sips this is fairly rare compared with  getting one good copy and one bad copy  so we we have this wide range of  ultra-rapid metabolizers extensive  metabolizers intermediate metabolizers  and poor metabolizers and as you can  imagine that will change how you respond  to a drug what type of dose can be  efficacious versus which one will not be  so if you're a poor metabolizer chances  are a small amount of a drug that goes  to that sip will be in your blood longer  circulating around and being active  longer because when you metabolize  things that's when they break down and  begin to be except severe side effects  the National Institute of Health has put  out a statement saying pharmacogenomics  is the 