s so there are when it  cannabinoid is a victim of a drug drug  interaction this is when cannabinoid  levels are changed by the presence of  another drug the drug is altering the  effects if you will of THC or CBD now  cannabinoids can also act as  perpetrators they can cause changes in  levels of another drug there's also  pharmacodynamic drug  interactions where both drugs contribute  to overlapping side-effects victim and  perpetrator don't apply here but an  overlapping side effect might be  sedation or increasing appetite or  something like that so there are a lot  of gaps in our knowledge about  cannabinoids and drug drug interactions  when they're taken orally  there's abilities but a lot of this  information is extrapolated from in  vitro drug metabolism studies that is a  petri dish test tube experiments as well  as animal studies we do have some data  available office which we will look at  but from these clinical studies what's  interesting is the drug drug  interactions was not the primary  objective of the study they just sort of  stumbled across it so here's an example  where cannabinoids are victims of drug  drug interactions so strong sip 3a4  inhibitors include a lot of these  plethora myosins ketoconazole and other  protease inhibitors these are very  commonly prescribed drugs when you look  at th see there's a 1.8 fold increase in  the presence of these inhibitors and an  11 hydroxy THC which is the liver  metabolites of THC someone take a THC  product orally and goes into the liver  and it's turned into 11 hydroxy THC  which is a bit more potent it's  considered to be a bit more potent than  regular old THC the levels and the blood  increased three and a half fold over the  cannabinoid alone and likewise CBD is  also affected saying almost a two-fold  increase and the amount in the blood  when administered with a drug that can't  inhibit this liver enzyme so like I said  THC is a potent active metabolite and  again this has been shown in research