ly  helping to make it sort of seamlessly  integrated as an option in clinical  study so in summary I strongly believe  that pharmacogenomics could be used to  help predict individual cannabinoid  metabolism helping to personalize  cannabis products for patients the  predictions from pharmacogenomic data  could speed up the trial and error  period improve dose rate raishin and  therapy outcomes drug interactions and  other factors such as diet must be taken  into account when you're either  designing clinical studies or using  cannabis products as a medicine it's  important to remember that cannabis is  greater than the sum of its parts  THC and CBD are interactions reduce side  effects and increase efficacy THC by  itself is kind of a lousy drug with some  side effects CBD and terpenes can help  reduce those side effects such as  intoxication and impairment genomic and  genetic responses influence THC and CBD  effects and and I hope this presentation  has been informative please feel free to  email any of your pharmacogenomic  questions to j Marku at IRC CMH org and  for any those of you who want to know  where some of this information comes  from here are some handy-dandy  references I'm happy to share these with  folks and for more information about the  Institute research opportunities please  contact our CEO Jane Roberts at Jay  Roberts at IRC CMH org thank you thank  you dr. Marc who for that informative  presentation I would also like to thank  lab roots for making today's educational  webcast  as a final reminder our speaker will  follow up with any questions you've  submitted via email that's all for now  thank you for joining us and we'll see  you next time bye
 

